<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Urraca of León and Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1079–<a href="/years/1126">1126</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Queen Urraca · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Urraca was Queen of León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> from 1109 to 1126, the daughter and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. Her second marriage, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a>, turned into civil war and was annulled, and she struggled throughout her reign against her husband, the Galician nobility and the supporters of her son. She was succeeded by that son of her first marriage, Alfonso VII.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile.jpg" alt="Urraca of León and Castile" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UrracaRegina_TumboA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Urraca was the only child of King Alfonso VI of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and his second wife, Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. The place and date of her birth are unknown, but she was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> or León around 1080. She married Raymond of Burgundy as part of Alfonso's diplomatic strategy of attracting alliances across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> inflicted a heavy defeat on him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Sagrajas</a> in 1086. Royal diplomas present the couple as rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> from about 1090, although Alfonso VI kept control of its administration.</p><p>In 1093 the birth of an illegitimate brother, Sancho Alfónsez, threatened her position as sole heir. Alfonso regarded Sancho as his heir, and by March 1107 he was elected king. Raymond died in September 1107, and Urraca took responsibility for the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, where her infant son Alfonso Raimúndez was seen as Raymond's lawful successor. Sancho died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ucles">Uclés</a> on 29 May 1108, which made Urraca her father's sole heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Urraca succeeded her father, who died on 30 June or 1 July 1109, as the first queen regnant in European history. Alfonso I, King of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, married her at the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> in October or November 1109. Two early chronicles say that Leonese aristocrats, convinced that a woman could not defend the kingdom against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, forced the marriage on her. It proved unhappy: she accused Alfonso of physical abuse and separated from him. Galician nobles led by Pedro Fróilaz de Traba proclaimed Alfonso Raimúndez king, and the conflicts grew into continuous civil war.</p><p>Urraca's lover and chief supporter Gómez González was killed at the Battle of Candespina in 1111, and Pedro González de Lara replaced him. By the autumn of 1112 a truce was brokered and the marriage annulled on grounds of consanguinity. Urraca recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, but Alfonso occupied a significant part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and her half-sister Theresa and Count Henry of Portugal held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>. Theresa later agreed to be Urraca's vassal in exchange for a vast territory in León.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the twelfth-century Chronicon Compostellanum, Urraca died in childbirth on 8 March 1126 in the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a>. The Historia Compostelana describes her as prudent, modest and sensible, but blames her failings on the weakness of women and calls her a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> for her liaisons with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>. Bernard F. Reilly notes that she remained in control of events and sees her solutions as pragmatic. Delaying the accession of her son Alfonso VII led to a turbulent transition, with the rebellion of Pedro González, an attempted invasion by the king of Aragon and renewed Galician revolts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Urraca of León and Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ferdinand divided his kingdoms among his sons in 1065, but Alfonso VI reunited León, Castile and Galicia by 1072, and they remained together under <b>Urraca</b> and Alfonso VII.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in León, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Castle of Saldaña, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=1109" aria-label="León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4415 649 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Urraca of León and Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4865 919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4865 919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=1109">What was here in 1109? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
